## Fixed at the implement gate: two fail-opens on the push path

Both seats BLOCKed round 1 and both independently found the same defect. It is
worth recording precisely, because it is the exact failure class this script
exists to prevent, committed while writing the script that prevents it.

**1. Check (b) passed a real gap clean.** The manifest was read as:

```sh
n_gates="$(git cat-file -p "$ref:manifest.json" 2>/dev/null | python3 -c '…' || echo -1)"
```

Under `set -o pipefail`, a failing `git cat-file` makes the whole substitution
non-zero *even though python already printed its `-1` sentinel* — so `|| echo -1`
appends a SECOND line, `n_gates` becomes `-1\n-1`, and that matches neither
`case` arm. A run ref with no `manifest.json` therefore exited **0, clean**, on a
hard check that gates every lane's push.

The 902-line predecessor got this right (`|| gates_count="error"` plus an
explicit test), so it was a regression I introduced, not an inherited hole. Fixed
by reading the blob into a variable first and classifying explicitly, so the
unreadable case and the invalid-JSON case are separate and neither can be
swallowed by a pipeline status.

**2. An unreachable origin was reported as unpushed refs.** `git ls-remote origin
… || true` turned any network, auth or remote failure into an empty origin map,
so every local ref became `absent from origin` and the audit exited 1. To the
operator the hook is addressing, exit 1 means "push your refs" and exit 2 means
"your environment is broken" — reporting the second as the first sends them to do
work that cannot possibly help. Now exits 2.

Both are pinned by regressions, and both regressions were negative-controlled by
reinstating the broken line and watching them fail (26 passed / 2 failed), then
restoring (28/28). The test had **no** bad-manifest coverage at all before this;
that omission is why the bug survived my own review.

Four smaller gate findings also folded: `--last 0` is now rejected as a usage
error rather than becoming an empty window; `-h` no longer truncates the header
mid-sentence; the allowlist parser no longer drops a final entry when the file
lacks a trailing newline; GAP lines return to stderr, where the predecessor put
them and where the hook's "see GAP lines above" refers; the hook's stale
`DOGFOOD_AUDIT_ETUDE_BIN` comment is gone (the new audit builds nothing); and
check (c) again skips superseded retro refs, which the first draft had silently
dropped.

